orkplace  i'm so grateful that those women afford  those battles  and that's why we can be here today and  i think for the experiences of  especially young women today you know  the world couldn't be  a better place there's real fantastic  opportunities for women  to work in whatever sphere they want to  earn as much money as men  to choose whether to have a family  work do both do one or the other  and i think it's so brilliant that women  have got all of these choices  i think the problem is our feminism has  kind of changed and not changed  to keep up with how the world's moved on  so  feminism still seems to have this  narrative that to be a woman is a really  terrible thing  that it's so depressing that the world's  out to get you  and that there's still so much  inequality in the world  and i think that's bad because it kind  of sends a message to women that  you know what there's almost no point  even trying because  things are going to be going against you  and the world's so tough and so hard and  you know there's going to be sexual  harassment you're going to be paid less  than men  everything's going to be really  difficult for you and on the one hand  that's just not true  but on the other hand it actually  becomes this terrible barrier  now that women have to confront this  idea that everything's going to be  against them um so then you look at  what's happened to feminism  i think too often it's really anti-men  in a very negative way which denies the  advantages that women have got so if you  look at higher education for example  excuse me for more women than men enter  university  far more women than men graduate this is  across all subject areas this isn't just  in one or two subjects  and yet the narrative on campus is often  about toxic masculinity  about how terrible men are as a problem  in the university and that's  such a shame you know i don't think for  young women men  are a problem and i think when you enter  the workplace men and women can  work as equals together men and women  set up families  together and i think this idea that men  are the problem women face  is just really really unhelpful  so my final point i'd make my final  concern with feminism  is i think too often it just becomes  middle-class concerns  if you look at the stories that we hear  about so often in the media  it's about women who work for the bbc or  women in hollywood  or women who want to be on boards of  directors  um women who are earning salaries that  for the majority of people in this  country  you know it's just unimaginable that you  would earn that much  money and yet it's these people were  asked  to be concerned about and i think the  two types of people  who are not in that kind of top five  percent of the population  feminism just has nothing to say i  imagine some people would argue that  whilst feminism has had its successes um  there's still  women are still fighting for abortion or  to get the vote and whilst  more women than men might go into  universities there's still less women  that are ceos  so there's like there's some sort of gap  that's happening so isn't there still  a need for feminism for  these struggles it's just that for some  people they've reached a point in which  these aren't struggles  yeah so that's a really really good  point so there are definitely places  around the world  where men and women do live very unequal  lives  so you talk about places where women  don't have the vote  so i'm guessing you're referring to some  countries around the middle east perhaps  but surprisingly feminism seems to have  very little to say  so when you see iranian women in muslim  countries or in muslim countries  kind of taking off their head scarves in  an act of rebellion  against what they see as an oppressive  religious practice  feminism tends to be very quiet about  that  and rather than coming out and really  championing these women who want to  rebel  against what they see as a patriarchal  religion  you know feminism kind of keeps quiet  and is a bit nervous  about appearing to be anti-muslim so  that's on the one hand  on the the abortion issue that you  raised yeah i think that's incredibly  important and i really really agree with  you  and you look at some of the um changes  that are happening at the moment in  america  um particularly in the southern states  where  abortion is practically becoming illegal  i mean  that if you put a six-week limit on it  most women don't know they're pregnant  at six weeks so it's practically making  abortion illegal  and that again is is i think appalling i  think abortion's so  important for women to have control of  their own fertility control of her own  bodies  but again you know feminism just doesn't  seem to be able to provide the challenge  that's needed to this legislation i mean  not all feminists by any stretch of the  imagination  but some feminists can't even agree on  what it means to be a woman  nowadays now if you can't agree that  being a woman means something in a  biological sense  that being a woman actually means you  have a certain biology  if we have to refer to pregnant people  for example rather than pregnant women  it makes it that much more difficult to  argue that it's  it's women who need access to abortion  um it's not  people who need access to abortion  that's one thing  and then you get what i think are really  quite ridiculous and backward things  that are being suggested to challenge  this like this idea that was raised by  one  actress and that women should go on sex  strike  until um the abortion regulation has  changed  and you're just like oh my god this is  the worst suggestion  ever because it kind of implies that sex  is something that women  don't enjoy just endure and kind of  give to men as an act of kind of  generosity  and it completely robs women of all  agency to actually say well  you know we enjoy sex just as much as  men we want to  have sex just as much as men it's 