se  arguments  if you deny that being a woman is a  biological reality  so does that mean that the onus is  placed on the  individual to fight for women's rights  rather than sort of the structural  forces that might uh hold back women's  rights  yeah no not at all not at all i would  actually ask for a movement  um that a political movement that that  goes beyond  just women that's kind of bigger than  feminism  so i i definitely don't think you can  make  you can make progress individually  although you know i think we all need to  accept that we have choices that we want  to we make we make choices about our own  lives  um we might not make them in  circumstances that we've chosen  but we all do have some choices that we  can make  each at each turn like say they might  not be the choices we want  but we all do have some choices but i  think the problem with feminism  is as a collective it's only ever faced  it only ever is focused on half of the  population  and actually think having a collective  politics that goes beyond half of the  population  is broader to argue for changes that are  in everybody's best interest  actually is really exciting because it  poses the potential for a much broader  a much bigger and collective approach to  politics  and what about those who say might  identify as a woman but they're not  biologically weren't born as a woman  would you be then isolating them from  fighting for women's rights  in the same way that perhaps like  feminism has isolated men from the  movement  um so for trans women i think  trans women can fight for their rights  and  if trans women want to fight for rights  they  can and should do that but i think it's  as trans women  rather than as women and um  i think a lot of trans women who you  speak to  actually agree with that point and um  don't want to be labelled or seen as  as natural women or born women or cis  women however you want to put it  and are quite conscious that that they  are not and that they don't want to  appropriate  the experience that women who were born  female  have had so i think trans women fighting  for trans women's rights  is great but they they don't need to um  label themselves as women in order to do  that  you talked about bringing men into the  conversation to do with women's rights  do you have sort of practical solutions  of ways of doing this or experience in  your own life in which you've managed to  sort of  bring the divide together um so i guess  and i suppose this is counter to what i  was saying about wanting collective  solutions because i guess this is just  my own experience but i think it's  experienced that's  shared more broadly by people in society  so  i have three children and obviously i've  had to negotiate  um work and often working stupidly long  hours or working away from home  with raising three children but i guess  the thing is that i haven't done that on  my own  and i'd be really lying um if i  pretended i'd done that on my own  it's been very much a joint effort with  my husband  as i think is not unusual i think  everybody every family um every relate  in every relationship  people do that they make those  negotiations uh there may have been  times in my own life where perhaps i've  done more of  looking after children and housework and  my husband's done more work  and then we've swapped around and i've  done more of the going out to work and  my husband's done more for looking  after children but i think it's not  um like a zero-sum game it's not like  we sit there with a tally chart and kind  of count  up you know well i've done this many  hours of  housework and looking after children and  you've had this amount of money  it's just a partnership and i don't  think  we're unusual in that i think that's how  everybody generally who i've come across  kind of gets on with their life people  muddle through and they do the best they  can  to try and make sure that the adults get  to do some of the things they